  "texts": {
    "preamble": "Ramlal vs State (2024:Rj-Jd:10573) on 21 February, 2024 Author: Kuldeep Mathur Bench: Kuldeep Mathur [2024:Rj-Jd:10573] High Court Of Judicature For Rajasthan At Jodhpur S.B. Criminal Revision Petition No. 939/2004 Ramlal S/o Nanda Ji, B/c Kalal, R/o Achalpur, Tehsil Pratapgarh, District Chittorgarh. ----Petitioner Versus State of Rajasthan. ----Respondent For Petitioner(s) : Mr. S.L. Jain For Respondent(s) : Mr. Arun Kumar, Pp Hon'Ble Mr. Justice Kuldeep Mathur Order 21/02/2024",
    "facts": "The petitioner was thereafter prosecuted for the offences under Section 7/16 of the Prevention of Food Adulteration Act, 1954.",
    "arguments": "Learned counsel for the petitioner submitted that incident in the present case relates to the year 1990. The petitioner does not have any criminal antecedent. Learned counsel vehemently submitted that after receiving report of public analysis prosecution sanction from the competent authority in Ramlal vs State (2024:Rj-Jd:10573) on 21 February, 2024 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/170365149/ 1 conformity with the provisions of the Prevention of Food Adulteration Act, 1954 was not taken. There is no positive evidence available on record that the petitioner is guilty of committing the alleged crime. Merely on the basis of the report of public analysis, it cannot be said that the petitioner had committed the alleged crime or the milk was adulterated. Learned counsel for the petitioner in the alternative submitted that the occurrence relates to the year 1990 and the petitioner has already served some parts of the sentence awarded to him. Therefore, the substantive sentence awarded to the petitioner may be reduced to the period already undergone by him. Reliance was placed on the judgment of the Hon'ble Supreme Court of India in the case of Puttaswamy v State of Karnataka: 2009 (1) WLC (SC) (Cri.) 623 and a judgment of Coordinate Bench of this Court in the case of Kamla Prasad v. State of Rajasthan: 2014 CrilLJ 2582. Per contra, learned Public Prosecutor submitted that there is no illegality or infirmity in the impugned judgment and therefore, the same do not call for any interference in exercise or revisional jurisdiction by this Court.",
    "petitioner_arguments": "Learned counsel for the petitioner submitted that incident in the present case relates to the year 1990. The petitioner does not have any criminal antecedent. Learned counsel vehemently submitted that after receiving report of public analysis prosecution sanction from the competent authority in Ramlal vs State (2024:Rj-Jd:10573) on 21 February, 2024 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/170365149/ 1 conformity with the provisions of the Prevention of Food Adulteration Act, 1954 was not taken. There is no positive evidence available on record that the petitioner is guilty of committing the alleged crime. Merely on the basis of the report of public analysis, it cannot be said that the petitioner had committed the alleged crime or the milk was adulterated. Learned counsel for the petitioner in the alternative submitted that the occurrence relates to the year 1990 and the petitioner has already served some parts of the sentence awarded to him. Therefore, the substantive sentence awarded to the petitioner may be reduced to the period already undergone by him.",
    "respondent_arguments": "Per contra, learned Public Prosecutor submitted that there is no illegality or infirmity in the impugned judgment and therefore, the same do not call for any interference in exercise or revisional jurisdiction by this Court.",
    "other_lawyer_arguments": "Reliance was placed on the judgment of the Hon'ble Supreme Court of India in the case of Puttaswamy v State of Karnataka: 2009 (1) WLC (SC) (Cri.) 623 and a judgment of Coordinate Bench of this Court in the case of Kamla Prasad v. State of Rajasthan: 2014 CrilLJ 2582."
  },
